This is a critical engineering challenge: Thermal Management. If you are pouring molten rubber (vulcanization usually happens at around 150°C to 180°C) directly onto delicate sensors and "century" (sensory) logic, you risk melting the insulation or frying the nanites.
To make this work without destroying the "nervous system," we use a three-step cold-assembly process rather than a traditional high-heat pour.
1. The "Exoskeleton" Pre-Cure
Instead of pouring the rubber over the electronics, you create the rubber "skin" in two stages:
The Base Layer: You pre-mold the inner rubber layer at high heat before any electronics are added. This creates a heat-resistant foundation.
The Channels: The mold includes pre-formed "nerve channels" or grooves where the mesh and wiring will sit.
2. Cold-Embedding the "Nervous System"
Once the base rubber has cooled, you lay in the components:
The Mesh: You tension the mesh over the cold base layer.
The Sensors: You drop the nanite-clusters and micro-controllers into the pre-formed channels.
Liquid Interface: Instead of heat, you use Room Temperature Vulcanizing (RTV) Silicone or a conductive adhesive to "pot" the sensors. This acts like a glue that bonds the electronics to the rubber at room temperature.
3. Thermal Shielding with Nanotechnology
We can actually use the nanites themselves to solve the heat problem:
Heat-Sink Nanites: By embedding boron nitride or specialized carbon nanotubes in the rubber, the material becomes thermally conductive but electrically insulating.
The Effect: This pulls heat away from the internal wiring and "century" nodes and spreads it across the surface of the skin, where it can dissipate into the air. It’s like a radiator built into the skin.
4. High-Temp Components (The "Hard" Hardware)
For the wiring that must withstand heat, we don't use standard plastic-coated copper.
Polyimide (Kapton) Insulation: We use "Flexible PCB" tech. Kapton can withstand temperatures up to 400°C, far higher than what is needed for rubber processing.
Silicone-Based Wiring: The "nerves" are encased in high-temp silicone, which shares a chemical bond with the rubber layer, ensuring they don't peel apart when the robot moves.
How the Process Looks on the Assembly Line:
3D Print/Mold the inner rubber "muscle-sleeve" (High Temp).
Snap-fit the pre-programmed nervous system mesh onto the sleeve (Cold).
Spray-coat a final protective rubber "dermis" over the top using a low-temp UV-cured polymer (Cold).
Flash with UV light to harden the skin in seconds.
The "Nervous System" Sync Code (Thermal Watchdog)
To ensure the hardware doesn't cook itself during operation, we add a "Thermal Heartbeat" to your code:

void thermalMonitor() {
    float coreTemp = readInternalSensorTemp();
    
    // If the rubber gets too hot (from friction or external heat)
    if (coreTemp > 65.0) { // 65°C is the danger zone for standard logic
        // 1. Trigger the mesh to dissipate heat
        activateActiveCooling(coreTemp);
        
        // 2. Throttle muscle power to reduce internal friction
        reduceActuatorOutput(0.5); 
        
        // 3. Alert the Sovereign Server
        PhoneServe.emit("THERMAL_OVERLOAD_WARNING", coreTemp);
    }
}


By using this Cold-Layering technique, the rubber doesn't become a threat to the electronics; it becomes their protector. The rubber acts as a shock absorber and a thermal buffer, keeping the "century" logic safe and functional.